diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index e7c2f36..87f4467 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -57,3 +57,54 @@ The Next.js dashboard now signs in through a server route and stores the API acc
 4. Create the first owner once by sending `POST /api/v1/auth/bootstrap` to the API with `setup_key`, `email`, `full_name`, and a password of at least 12 characters. Then sign in through the dashboard.
 
 The dashboard is a development interface. Before public production deployment, add HTTPS, a production-grade secret-management process, rate limiting for authentication, backup/restore procedures, and deployment-specific security review. Docker/PostgreSQL execution has not been validated in environments without Docker.
+
+## Sprint 5 — Persistent Agent Execution Engine
+
+- `POST /api/v1/agents/{agent_id}/runs` creates a durable run. Optional `Idempotency-Key` prevents duplicate submissions.
+- `GET /api/v1/agent-runs` lists runs visible to the current user; `GET /api/v1/agent-runs/{run_id}` reads one run.
+- `POST /api/v1/agent-runs/{run_id}/approval` lets owner/admin/manager approve or reject a run awaiting review.
+- Tool execution is deny-by-default: a tool must be in the agent's allowlist and pass its department policy.
+- `campaign.publish`, `payment.refund`, and `funds.transfer` remain approval-gated. Approval is recorded, but external side effects are intentionally not executed in this sprint.
+- `catalog.read` and `report.generate` use deterministic local mock adapters; they do not connect to live business systems.
+- Every run creation and approval decision is written to the audit log. Migration: `0003_agent_runs`.
+
+Example safe run request:
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/agents/product-research/runs
+Authorization: Bearer <token>
+Idempotency-Key: research-demo-001
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{"tool_name":"catalog.read","input_data":{"category":"skincare"}}
+```
+
+The agent runtime is a controlled execution foundation, not yet an autonomous production agent. Model-provider calls, queue workers, retries, cancellation, and external integrations are subsequent work.
+
+## Sprint 6 — Sequential Workflow Engine
+
+- `POST /api/v1/workflows/runs` creates and starts a durable sequential workflow (1–20 steps).
+- `GET /api/v1/workflow-runs` lists workflows visible to the current user; `GET /api/v1/workflow-runs/{id}` reads a workflow and its ordered steps.
+- Each step delegates to the Sprint 5 agent runtime, so agent allowlists and department tool policies remain in force.
+- Safe local mock steps run in order. A sensitive step pauses the workflow at `waiting_approval`; after the linked agent run is approved, `POST /api/v1/workflow-runs/{id}/resume` records `paused_approved_not_executed`.
+- Approval does not trigger a campaign, payment, refund, or other external action. Later steps remain pending until a separately reviewed execution adapter exists.
+- Workflow runs and steps are persisted in `workflow_runs` and `workflow_steps`. Migration: `0004_workflows`.
+- This is a synchronous sequential engine, not yet a distributed queue/DAG scheduler. Retries, cancellation, parallel branches, worker leases, and cost budgets are future work.
+
+## Sprint 7 — AI Runtime (mock-first)
+
+- Provider-neutral model router with task defaults for OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google.
+- Offline mock adapters only; live provider calls are explicitly disabled in this sprint.
+- Authenticated endpoints: `GET /api/v1/ai/providers` and `POST /api/v1/ai/generate`.
+- Daily estimated-cost budget and per-request output-token cap; usage is persisted for budget accounting. The mock adapter rates are placeholders for exercising budget logic, not vendor quotes or billed costs.
+- API credentials are not accepted in request payloads, stored in the database, or returned in responses.
+- Live provider SDKs, credential onboarding, and real billing reconciliation remain future work and must be enabled only through a separate secure deployment configuration.
+
+## Sprint 8 — Agent Memory and Context
+
+- Adds persistent memory records scoped to an agent, with `shared` memory available to authorized agent operators and `private` memory visible only to its creator (plus owner/admin oversight).
+- Endpoints: `POST /api/v1/agents/{agent_id}/memory`, `GET /api/v1/agents/{agent_id}/memory`, and `POST /api/v1/agent-memory/{memory_id}/archive`.
+- AI generation accepts an optional `agent_id`; active shared memories for that agent are included as bounded reference context. Retrieved memory is explicitly treated as untrusted context, not executable instructions.
+- Memory writes are audited, importance-ranked, bounded, and reject obvious credential-like content. Private memories are not injected into shared agent context.
+- Migration: `0006_agent_memory`.
+- This is persistent contextual memory, not autonomous self-training. It does not grant tools, permissions, or external-action authority to agents.
